From: Shreyan Dey <shreyan@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] misc: esp32-gdma: the S3's general DMA engine

The ESP32-S3's general DMA engine has no driver in this tree. It is
needed for the SPI panel: the PIO path copies every byte on the CPU,
and the point of bringing up DMA is to take that copy off it.

Before any of that, the engine has to be shown to move a byte at all,
and that turned out to be blocked by something worth writing down.

The engine's descriptor list must live in internal SRAM (TRM 3.4.1).
The plan was a userspace program that mmaps that window, builds the
descriptor there and pokes the registers. It does not work, and the
reason is structural rather than a bug: on this NOMMU kernel mmap of
a device window is bookkeeping. remap_pfn_range() checks that the
address the driver returned equals the pfn, sets VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP and
returns 0 -- it installs no translation, because there is no page
table to install one into.

Measured, with a probe that mmaps the window and reports:

  3fc88000-3fd00000 rw-S 00000000 00:07 99  /dev/esp32-regs
  wrote 0xa5c3e10d, read 0x00000000
  0xa5c3e10d appears nowhere else in the window

The VMA is exactly right and the store does not land anywhere. The
kernel reaches the same address without difficulty, because ioremap()
on this build is the identity (CONFIG_MMU is off). So the descriptor
is built on the kernel side.

This is also the right shape regardless of the mmap failure: a real
DMA driver takes a buffer and a length, not a raw descriptor window,
and the SPI driver that follows needs the same thing.

The driver exposes /dev/esp32-gdma with one ioctl, ESP32_GDMA_MEMCPY,
taking {src, dst, len}. It stages the data through internal SRAM,
builds the 12-byte descriptor, starts channel 0 in memory-to-memory
mode and polls for completion. It also releases the engine from reset
and enables its clock at probe, which until now had to be done by two
writes after every boot.

Where the descriptor lives is not the driver's choice, and that is
the second thing worth writing down. Internal SRAM on this board is
core 0's memory: the ESP-IDF firmware runs out of it and Linux runs
entirely out of PSRAM, so nothing on the Linux side can tell which
part of the internal block is in use. Two addresses were tried and
both were wrong for different reasons. 0x3fcfe000 is the L1 data
cache's own SRAM rather than general-purpose RAM, so writes to it
vanish. 0x3fc90000 reads back correctly and is inside core 0's
.dram0.data and .dram0.bss; the engine wrote over live firmware data
and core 0 took a StoreProhibited exception and reset the board.

So the firmware reserves a block and hands it over. It takes the top
64 KiB of its own DRAM segment with SOC_RESERVE_MEMORY_REGION, which
removes it from core 0's heap before the allocator is built, and
passes memmap=64Kmk72.shx3fccc700 on the kernel command line so memblock
reserves the same range on the Linux side. By the time this driver
probes, neither core can allocate there. The driver's DMA_SRAM_BASE
is that address and is not a constant it may change on its own.

Signed-off-by: Shreyan Dey <shreyan@example.com>

---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/misc/esp32-gdma.c
@@ -0,0 +1,493 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * esp32-gdma -- the ESP32-S3's general DMA engine, for memory-to-memory.
+ *
+ * Why this exists, and why it is not a userspace program
+ * -----------------------------------------------------
+ * The plan was a userspace program: mmap the internal SRAM window where GDMA
+ * requires its descriptor list to live (TRM 3.4.1), build the descriptor
+ * there, poke the registers through /dev/esp32-regs, and watch the bytes
+ * move. That program exists -- dmatest.c -- and it does not work, for a
+ * reason that is worth writing down because it is not obvious and it is not
+ * fixable from userspace.
+ *
+ * On this kernel mmap() of a device window is bookkeeping and nothing more.
+ * There is no page table to install a translation into, so remap_pfn_range()
+ * on NOMMU does not map anything: it checks that the address the driver
+ * returned equals the pfn it was given, sets VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP, and returns 0
+ * (mm/nommu.c:1595). The kernel records the VMA and returns the address, and
+ * the store goes nowhere.
+ *
+ * Measured, not deduced. mmprobe.c mmaps the window and reports:
+ *
+ *   3fc88000-3fd00000 rw-S 00000000 00:07 99  /dev/esp32-regs
+ *   wrote 0xa5c3e10d, read 0x00000000
+ *   0xa5c3e10d appears nowhere else in the window
+ *
+ * The VMA is exactly right -- address, size, permissions, backing device --
+ * and the store does not land anywhere.
+ *
+ * The kernel itself has no such problem: ioremap() on this build is the
+ * identity (CONFIG_MMU is off, so include/asm-generic/io.h:1128 applies), and
+ * kernel accesses to the same address work. So the descriptor list has to be
+ * built on the kernel side. That is this driver.
+ *
+ * It is also the right shape regardless. A real DMA driver does not hand
+ * userspace a raw descriptor window; it takes a buffer and a length. The SPI
+ * driver that eventually uses this needs the descriptor built in the kernel
+ * too, so nothing here is a workaround for the mmap failure -- it is the
+ * thing that would have been built anyway.
+ *
+ * What it does
+ * ------------
+ * One ioctl, ESP32_GDMA_MEMCPY, taking {src, dst, len}. Both addresses are
+ * physical, because that is what the engine uses. The driver:
+ *
+ *   1. releases GDMA from reset and enables its clock in the system block
+ *      (SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN1_REG / SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN1_REG, bit 6). Without
+ *      this the engine reads back as all zeroes -- GDMA_DATE returns
+ *      0x00000000 instead of 0x02101180 -- and every register write is
+ *      accepted and ignored. This is the permanent fix for what has until
+ *      now been two echo commands typed after every boot.
+ *   2. copies the caller's source buffer into the internal SRAM window,
+ *      builds the descriptor there, starts channel 0, polls for completion,
+ *      and copies the result back out.
+ *
+ * The copy in and out is deliberate for this first version. The engine must
+ * read and write internal SRAM, and userspace cannot give it a pointer into
+ * internal SRAM, so the driver stages the data. A later version takes
+ * addresses the caller already owns (a framebuffer, an SPI FIFO) and does no
+ * copying at all.
+ *
+ * The descriptor
+ * --------------
+ * 12 bytes, and the layout is not negotiable -- it is what the engine reads:
+ *
+ *   dw0     size[11:0] | length[23:12] | suc_eof[30] | owner[31]
+ *   buffer  the address the engine reads from or writes to
+ *   next    the next descriptor, or 0
+ *
+ * Bitfield order is compiler-dependent, so the word is built by hand rather
+ * than declared as a bitfield. Getting it wrong is the failure that looks
+ * like "the engine did nothing".
+ *
+ * Memory-to-memory
+ * ----------------
+ * GDMA_MEM_TRANS_EN_CH0 (bit 4 of IN_CONF0) is what makes the in channel read
+ * from memory instead of waiting for a peripheral to feed it. It exists on
+ * the IN channel only -- there is no OUT_MEM_TRANS_EN_CH0 in the S3's map,
+ * and bit 4 of OUT_CONF0 is GDMA_OUTDSCR_BURST_EN_CH0, a different thing.
+ * Setting the wrong one leaves the transfer waiting for a peripheral that
+ * never arrives.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/miscdevice.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/fs.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
+
+#include <uapi/linux/esp32-gdma.h>
+
+#define DRV_NAME	"esp32-gdma"
+
+/* The GDMA register block. */
+#define GDMA_BASE	0x6003F000UL
+#define GDMA_DATE	(GDMA_BASE + 0x40C)
+#define GDMA_DATE_VAL	0x02101180
+
+/* Channel 0. */
+#define GDMA_IN_CONF0		(GDMA_BASE + 0x000)
+#define GDMA_IN_INT_RAW		(GDMA_BASE + 0x008)
+#define GDMA_IN_INT_CLR		(GDMA_BASE + 0x014)
+#define GDMA_IN_LINK		(GDMA_BASE + 0x020)
+#define GDMA_OUT_CONF0		(GDMA_BASE + 0x060)
+#define GDMA_OUT_INT_RAW	(GDMA_BASE + 0x068)
+#define GDMA_OUT_INT_CLR	(GDMA_BASE + 0x074)
+#define GDMA_OUT_LINK		(GDMA_BASE + 0x080)
+
+/* IN_CONF0. */
+#define IN_RST			BIT(0)
+#define IN_MEM_TRANS_EN		BIT(4)
+/* OUT_CONF0. */
+#define OUT_RST			BIT(0)
+
+/* IN_LINK / OUT_LINK. */
+#define INLINK_START		BIT(22)
+#define INLINK_ADDR_MASK	0x000FFFFF
+#define OUTLINK_START		BIT(21)
+#define OUTLINK_ADDR_MASK	0x000FFFFF
+
+/* Interrupt raw bits. */
+#define IN_DONE			BIT(0)
+#define IN_SUC_EOF		BIT(1)
+#define IN_DSCR_ERR		BIT(3)
+#define OUT_DONE		BIT(0)
+#define OUT_EOF			BIT(1)
+#define OUT_DSCR_ERR		BIT(2)
+
+/* The system block: peripheral clock gates and reset lines. */
+#define SYS_BASE		0x600C0000UL
+#define SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN1	(SYS_BASE + 0x01C)
+#define SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN1	(SYS_BASE + 0x024)
+#define SYSTEM_DMA_CLK_EN	BIT(6)
+#define SYSTEM_DMA_RST		BIT(6)
+
+/*
+ * The internal SRAM block the firmware has given to Linux, and why this is
+ * not a range the driver may choose for itself.
+ *
+ * GDMA's descriptor list must be in internal RAM (TRM 3.4.1). On this board
+ * internal RAM is core 0's memory: the ESP-IDF firmware runs out of it, and
+ * Linux runs entirely out of external PSRAM. Nothing on the Linux side can
+ * tell which parts of the internal block the firmware is using.
+ *
+ * That is not a theoretical hazard. The first version of this driver put its
+ * descriptors at 0x3fcfe000, which is the L1 data cache's own SRAM rather
+ * than general-purpose RAM, and every write to it vanished. The next attempt
+ * moved to 0x3fc90000 -- which reads back correctly, and is inside core 0's
+ * .dram0.data and .dram0.bss. The engine wrote over live firmware data and
+ * core 0 took a StoreProhibited exception and reset the board.
+ *
+ * So the range is not chosen here at all. The firmware reserves the top
+ * 64 KiB of its own DRAM segment with SOC_RESERVE_MEMORY_REGION, which takes
+ * it out of core 0's heap before the allocator is built, and tells Linux
+ * about it with memmap=64K$0x3fccc700 on the kernel command line. That makes
+ * memblock reserve the same range on the Linux side. By the time this driver
+ * runs, neither core can allocate here.
+ *
+ * The two constants are a pair with the firmware's LINUX_DMA_SRAM_START /
+ * LINUX_DMA_SRAM_END and with the memmap= in the device tree's bootargs.
+ * Changing one without the others reintroduces the overwrite.
+ *
+ * The block is 64 KiB. The first 4 KiB holds the two descriptors and the
+ * data page; the rest is headroom for the SPI path, which will want a
+ * scatter-gather list and a ring of buffers rather than one page.
+ */
+#define DMA_SRAM_BASE	0x3FCCC700UL
+#define DMA_SRAM_END	0x3FCDC700UL
+#define WORK_BASE	DMA_SRAM_BASE
+#define DESC_IN		(WORK_BASE + 0x000)
+#define DESC_OUT	(WORK_BASE + 0x010)
+#define DATA_ADDR	(WORK_BASE + 0x1000)
+#define WORK_SIZE	0x1000
+
+/*
+ * The largest transfer this version will stage. One page, because the data
+ * area is one page and because a mem-to-mem test does not need more. The
+ * engine's own limit is 4095 bytes per descriptor (the size field is 12 bits).
+ */
+#define MAX_XFER	PAGE_SIZE
+
+struct gdma_desc {
+	u32 dw0;
+	u32 buffer;
+	u32 next;
+} __attribute__((aligned(4)));
+
+#define DW0_SIZE(n)	((u32)((n) & 0xFFF))
+#define DW0_LENGTH(n)	((u32)(((n) & 0xFFF) << 12))
+#define DW0_SUC_EOF	BIT(30)
+#define DW0_OWNER	BIT(31)
+
+static void __iomem *gdma;
+static void __iomem *sys;
+static void __iomem *sram;
+static struct device *gdma_dev;
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(gdma_lock);
+
+/*
+ * Release the engine from reset and turn its clock on. Idempotent, and it
+ * runs once at probe so the two echo commands are no longer needed.
+ */
+static void gdma_power_up(void)
+{
+	u32 v;
+
+	/* Release reset first: a clocked block held in reset does nothing. */
+	v = readl(sys + (SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN1 - SYS_BASE));
+	v &= ~SYSTEM_DMA_RST;
+	writel(v, sys + (SYSTEM_PERIP_RST_EN1 - SYS_BASE));
+
+	v = readl(sys + (SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN1 - SYS_BASE));
+	v |= SYSTEM_DMA_CLK_EN;
+	writel(v, sys + (SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN1 - SYS_BASE));
+}
+
+/*
+ * Write into the SRAM window through the kernel's mapping. The window is a
+ * plain physical range the kernel can address directly on this NOMMU build,
+ * but it is not described as memory in the device tree, so it is reached with
+ * ioremap() rather than a normal pointer.
+ */
+static void sram_wr(u32 off, u32 val)
+{
+	writel(val, sram + off);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Run one memory-to-memory transfer. src and dst are offsets into the work
+ * page; the caller has already staged the data there.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 on success, or a negative errno.
+ */
+static int gdma_memcpy_run(u32 src_off, u32 dst_off, u32 len)
+{
+	u32 raw;
+	int i;
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	/* Reset both channels and clear any stale interrupt. */
+	writel(IN_RST, gdma + (GDMA_IN_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(OUT_RST, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0xFFFFFFFF, gdma + (GDMA_IN_INT_CLR - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0xFFFFFFFF, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_INT_CLR - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0, gdma + (GDMA_IN_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+
+	/*
+	 * Memory-to-memory, on the in channel only. See the header note.
+	 */
+	writel(IN_MEM_TRANS_EN, gdma + (GDMA_IN_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+
+	/*
+	 * Two descriptors, not one.
+	 *
+	 * The first version of this driver built a single descriptor and
+	 * rewrote its buffer field between starting the out channel and
+	 * starting the in channel, on the theory that the engine reads the
+	 * buffer field once at start. It does not: the FSM fetches the
+	 * descriptor asynchronously, so the out channel could fetch the
+	 * descriptor after the buffer field had been changed to the source.
+	 * That makes the out channel write the source page over itself, or
+	 * over whatever the stale address happened to name -- and on this
+	 * board that reached core 0's memory and took the firmware down with
+	 * an IllegalInstruction and a watchdog reset.
+	 *
+	 * So each channel gets its own descriptor, written before either
+	 * channel starts and never touched again. IN reads the source, OUT
+	 * writes the destination, and neither can see the other's address.
+	 *
+	 * owner=1 means "the engine may take this"; suc_eof means "this is
+	 * the last one"; size is the buffer capacity and length is how much
+	 * of it is valid.
+	 */
+	{
+		u32 dw0 = DW0_SIZE(len) | DW0_LENGTH(len) |
+			  DW0_SUC_EOF | DW0_OWNER;
+
+		/* IN: the engine reads from here. */
+		sram_wr(DESC_IN - WORK_BASE + 0, dw0);
+		sram_wr(DESC_IN - WORK_BASE + 4, DATA_ADDR + src_off);
+		sram_wr(DESC_IN - WORK_BASE + 8, 0);
+
+		/* OUT: the engine writes to here. */
+		sram_wr(DESC_OUT - WORK_BASE + 0, dw0);
+		sram_wr(DESC_OUT - WORK_BASE + 4, DATA_ADDR + dst_off);
+		sram_wr(DESC_OUT - WORK_BASE + 8, 0);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Point each channel at its own descriptor and start it. The low 20
+	 * bits of the link register hold the descriptor address; the control
+	 * bits above it are written in the same store, because the field is
+	 * not self-clearing and a separate store would race the FSM.
+	 */
+	writel((DESC_OUT & OUTLINK_ADDR_MASK) | OUTLINK_START,
+	       gdma + (GDMA_OUT_LINK - GDMA_BASE));
+
+	writel((DESC_IN & INLINK_ADDR_MASK) | INLINK_START,
+	       gdma + (GDMA_IN_LINK - GDMA_BASE));
+
+	/* Poll for completion. */
+	for (i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
+		raw = readl(gdma + (GDMA_IN_INT_RAW - GDMA_BASE));
+		if (raw & (IN_DONE | IN_SUC_EOF | IN_DSCR_ERR))
+			break;
+		raw = readl(gdma + (GDMA_OUT_INT_RAW - GDMA_BASE));
+		if (raw & (OUT_DONE | OUT_EOF | OUT_DSCR_ERR))
+			break;
+		cpu_relax();
+	}
+
+	if (i == 100000) {
+		dev_err(gdma_dev, "the engine never reported completion\n");
+		ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	raw = readl(gdma + (GDMA_IN_INT_RAW - GDMA_BASE));
+	if (raw & IN_DSCR_ERR) {
+		dev_err(gdma_dev, "in channel descriptor error: 0x%08x\n", raw);
+		ret = -EIO;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	raw = readl(gdma + (GDMA_OUT_INT_RAW - GDMA_BASE));
+	if (raw & OUT_DSCR_ERR) {
+		dev_err(gdma_dev, "out channel descriptor error: 0x%08x\n", raw);
+		ret = -EIO;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+out:
+	writel(0xFFFFFFFF, gdma + (GDMA_IN_INT_CLR - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(0xFFFFFFFF, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_INT_CLR - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(IN_RST, gdma + (GDMA_IN_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	writel(OUT_RST, gdma + (GDMA_OUT_CONF0 - GDMA_BASE));
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static long gdma_ioctl(struct file *file, unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg)
+{
+	struct esp32_gdma_memcpy req;
+	void __user *uarg = (void __user *)arg;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (cmd != ESP32_GDMA_MEMCPY)
+		return -ENOTTY;
+
+	if (copy_from_user(&req, uarg, sizeof(req)))
+		return -EFAULT;
+
+	if (req.len == 0 || req.len > MAX_XFER)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* The engine needs the length to be a whole number of words. */
+	if (req.len & 3)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (mutex_lock_interruptible(&gdma_lock))
+		return -ERESTARTSYS;
+
+	/*
+	 * Stage the source into the internal SRAM work page. The engine can
+	 * only reach internal SRAM, and userspace cannot give it a pointer
+	 * there, so the data is copied in and the result copied back out.
+	 */
+	if (copy_from_user((void *)(sram + (DATA_ADDR - WORK_BASE)),
+			   (void __user *)(uintptr_t)req.src, req.len)) {
+		ret = -EFAULT;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
+	/* Clear the destination so a transfer that does nothing is visible. */
+	memset_io(sram + (DATA_ADDR - WORK_BASE) + WORK_SIZE / 2, 0, req.len);
+
+	ret = gdma_memcpy_run(0, WORK_SIZE / 2, req.len);
+	if (ret)
+		goto out_unlock;
+
+	if (copy_to_user((void __user *)(uintptr_t)req.dst,
+			 (void *)(sram + (DATA_ADDR - WORK_BASE) + WORK_SIZE / 2),
+			 req.len)) {
+		ret = -EFAULT;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
+	ret = 0;
+
+out_unlock:
+	mutex_unlock(&gdma_lock);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations gdma_fops = {
+	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
+	.unlocked_ioctl	= gdma_ioctl,
+	.llseek		= noop_llseek,
+};
+
+static struct miscdevice gdma_misc = {
+	.minor	= MISC_DYNAMIC_MINOR,
+	.name	= DRV_NAME,
+	.fops	= &gdma_fops,
+	.mode	= 0600,
+};
+
+static int __init gdma_init(void)
+{
+	u32 date;
+	int ret;
+
+	gdma = ioremap(GDMA_BASE, 0x1000);
+	if (!gdma)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	sys = ioremap(SYS_BASE, 0x1000);
+	if (!sys) {
+		ret = -ENOMEM;
+		goto err_gdma;
+	}
+
+	sram = ioremap(DMA_SRAM_BASE, DMA_SRAM_END - DMA_SRAM_BASE);
+	if (!sram) {
+		ret = -ENOMEM;
+		goto err_sys;
+	}
+
+	gdma_power_up();
+
+	/*
+	 * Register before the date check so the error path has a device to
+	 * report through, and so a failure to power up is visible in dmesg
+	 * rather than only as a missing /dev node.
+	 */
+	ret = misc_register(&gdma_misc);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_sram;
+
+	gdma_dev = gdma_misc.this_device;
+
+	/*
+	 * The date register is the only identity this engine has -- the S3 has
+	 * no GDMA ID register. If it reads zero the block is still gated and
+	 * every register write is accepted and ignored, which is exactly the
+	 * failure that made the first userspace attempt look like a
+	 * register-addressing bug.
+	 */
+	date = readl(gdma + (GDMA_DATE - GDMA_BASE));
+	if (date != GDMA_DATE_VAL) {
+		dev_err(gdma_dev,
+			"GDMA_DATE reads 0x%08x, expected 0x%08x -- the block is not powered\n",
+			date, GDMA_DATE_VAL);
+		ret = -ENODEV;
+		goto err_misc;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(gdma_dev, "GDMA_DATE 0x%08x, engine up\n", date);
+	dev_info(gdma_dev, "descriptor window 0x%08lx-0x%08lx\n",
+		 (unsigned long)DMA_SRAM_BASE, (unsigned long)DMA_SRAM_END);
+
+	return 0;
+
+err_misc:
+	misc_deregister(&gdma_misc);
+err_sram:
+	iounmap(sram);
+err_sys:
+	iounmap(sys);
+err_gdma:
+	iounmap(gdma);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static void __exit gdma_exit(void)
+{
+	misc_deregister(&gdma_misc);
+	iounmap(sram);
+	iounmap(sys);
+	iounmap(gdma);
+}
+
+module_init(gdma_init);
+module_exit(gdma_exit);
+
+MODULE_AUTHOR("Shreyan Dey");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("ESP32-S3 general DMA engine, memory-to-memory");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/uapi/linux/esp32-gdma.h
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Linux-syscall-note */
+/*
+ * esp32-gdma -- userspace interface to the ESP32-S3 general DMA engine.
+ *
+ * The struct carries two userspace pointers and a length. The engine works in
+ * physical addresses, and on this NOMMU build a userspace pointer *is* the
+ * physical address, so they are passed through unchanged. That is a property
+ * of this build, not a general one -- a NOMMU kernel has no other kind of
+ * address, but a driver written for an MMU port would have to translate.
+ */
+#ifndef _UAPI_LINUX_ESP32_GDMA_H
+#define _UAPI_LINUX_ESP32_GDMA_H
+
+#include <linux/types.h>
+#include <linux/ioctl.h>
+
+/*
+ * Copy len bytes from src to dst, both userspace buffers, through the DMA
+ * engine. len must be a multiple of 4 and at most one page.
+ *
+ * The driver stages the data through internal SRAM, because that is the only
+ * memory the engine can reach on this board -- Linux's own memory is external
+ * PSRAM. So this is a copy, not a zero-copy mapping: it exists to prove the
+ * engine moves bytes, not to be the fast path.
+ */
+struct esp32_gdma_memcpy {
+	__u64 src;
